// ── the firmware's own channel map, which is where the channel fact actually lives ─────────────

// THE ALTRA BOOT FIRMWARE PRINTS ITS DRAM TRAINING RESULT to the serial console before any OS
// exists, one line per populated module, naming socket, memory controller and slot-within-channel
// directly: "SK0 MC5 S0: RDIMM[ad:80] 16GB 2666 ECC 2R x8 RCD[32:86] HMA82GR7CJR8N-VK". This is
// the only channel-resolved reading available on this platform, and it is strictly better than
// what EDAC would have given: it names the controller AND the slot position within the channel,
// which a csrow tree does not.
//
// EVERY POPULATED MODULE SITS AT S0 — slot 0 of its channel — on all sixteen channels, so the unit
// is uniformly 1DPC and the sixteen empty connectors are the S1 position of each channel.
// NO CONNECTOR FIELD, AND ITS ABSENCE IS THE POINT. Firmware DRAM training reports a SOCKET, a
// MEMORY CONTROLLER and a SLOT WITHIN THAT CHANNEL. It does not report a J-number, and neither does
// SMBIOS. The J-number for a given (socket, controller) at 1DPC is already derived, from the
// guide's pair and population tables, by
// extdeps.ampere.mt_collins_product_brief.memory_population -- so carrying a connector column here
// was a SECOND COPY of that mapping, hand-written beside the authority that owns it and free to
// drift from it (DESIGN section 3, one fact one place; section 2, net concepts must not grow by
// re-invention). Review 63939 found it, along with this module's claim to "consume" that authority
// while importing nothing from it.
//
// The census now carries only what the firmware SAID. The join to a connector is performed where
// it can be checked, by the witness, against the upstream rows themselves.
type MtCollins1FirmwareChannelBinding {
  processor_socket: Nat
  memory_controller: Nat
  slot_in_channel: Nat
  rank_count: Nat
  chip_width: BitWidth
  part_number: NonEmptyStr
}

data mtcollins1_firmware_channel_bindings: List<MtCollins1FirmwareChannelBinding> = [
  MtCollins1FirmwareChannelBinding { processor_socket: 0, memory_controller: 0, slot_in_channel: 0, rank_count: 1, chip_width: bit_width(4), part_number: "HMA82GR7AFR4N-VK" },
  MtCollins1FirmwareChannelBinding { processor_socket: 0, memory_controller: 1, slot_in_channel: 0, rank_count: 2, chip_width: bit_width(8), part_number: "HMA82GR7AFR8N-VK" },
  MtCollins1FirmwareChannelBinding { processor_socket: 0, memory_controller: 2, slot_in_channel: 0, rank_count: 1, chip_width: bit_width(4), part_number: "HMA82GR7AFR4N-VK" },
  MtCollins1FirmwareChannelBinding { processor_socket: 0, memory_controller: 3, slot_in_channel: 0, rank_count: 1, chip_width: bit_width(4), part_number: "HMA82GR7AFR4N-VK" },
  MtCollins1FirmwareChannelBinding { processor_socket: 0, memory_controller: 4, slot_in_channel: 0, rank_count: 2, chip_width: bit_width(8), part_number: "HMA82GR7AFR8N-VK" },
  MtCollins1FirmwareChannelBinding { processor_socket: 0, memory_controller: 5, slot_in_channel: 0, rank_count: 2, chip_width: bit_width(8), part_number: "HMA82GR7CJR8N-VK" },
  MtCollins1FirmwareChannelBinding { processor_socket: 0, memory_controller: 6, slot_in_channel: 0, rank_count: 1, chip_width: bit_width(4), part_number: "HMA82GR7AFR4N-VK" },
  MtCollins1FirmwareChannelBinding { processor_socket: 0, memory_controller: 7, slot_in_channel: 0, rank_count: 2, chip_width: bit_width(8), part_number: "HMA82GR7AFR8N-VK" },
  MtCollins1FirmwareChannelBinding { processor_socket: 1, memory_controller: 0, slot_in_channel: 0, rank_count: 1, chip_width: bit_width(4), part_number: "HMA82GR7AFR4N-VK" },
  MtCollins1FirmwareChannelBinding { processor_socket: 1, memory_controller: 1, slot_in_channel: 0, rank_count: 1, chip_width: bit_width(4), part_number: "HMA82GR7AFR4N-VK" },
  MtCollins1FirmwareChannelBinding { processor_socket: 1, memory_controller: 2, slot_in_channel: 0, rank_count: 2, chip_width: bit_width(8), part_number: "HMA82GR7AFR8N-VK" },
  MtCollins1FirmwareChannelBinding { processor_socket: 1, memory_controller: 3, slot_in_channel: 0, rank_count: 1, chip_width: bit_width(4), part_number: "HMA82GR7AFR4N-VK" },
  MtCollins1FirmwareChannelBinding { processor_socket: 1, memory_controller: 4, slot_in_channel: 0, rank_count: 1, chip_width: bit_width(4), part_number: "HMA82GR7AFR4N-VK" },
  MtCollins1FirmwareChannelBinding { processor_socket: 1, memory_controller: 5, slot_in_channel: 0, rank_count: 1, chip_width: bit_width(4), part_number: "HMA82GR7AFR4N-VK" },
  MtCollins1FirmwareChannelBinding { processor_socket: 1, memory_controller: 6, slot_in_channel: 0, rank_count: 1, chip_width: bit_width(4), part_number: "HMA82GR7AFR4N-VK" },
  MtCollins1FirmwareChannelBinding { processor_socket: 1, memory_controller: 7, slot_in_channel: 0, rank_count: 2, chip_width: bit_width(8), part_number: "HMA82GR7AFR8N-VK" },
]

// ── how connector numbers were attached to the two readings, and what discriminates the join ───

// NEITHER READING PRINTS A J-NUMBER. The firmware names (socket, controller, slot-in-channel);
// SMBIOS names "DIMM n". The connector field on both lists above is therefore a JOIN, and it is
// declared as one rather than presented as a direct reading.
//
// THE UPSTREAM SIDE IS ALREADY MODELLED AND IS NOT RE-TRANSCRIBED HERE.
// extdeps.ampere.mt_collins_product_brief.memory_population carries, from Tables 12-13 of Ampere's
// Getting Started Guide, that connectors pair as {J1,J2}, {J3,J4} … {J31,J32} with pair k on MCU k,
// that J1..J16 serve socket 0 and J17..J32 serve socket 1, and that the 16-DIMM 1DPC population
// occupies the odd member of every pair. This module consumes that and adds no second copy of it.
//
// THE JOIN IS: SMBIOS "DIMM n" is connector Jn on socket 0 and J(16+n) on socket 1, taken in the
// order the SMBIOS Type 17 structures appear.
//
// WHAT DISCRIMINATES IT, which matters because an ordering hypothesis that merely "looks right"
// establishes nothing. Three independent checks, and the first is the sharp one:
//
//   1. A UNIQUE PART LANDS ON A UNIQUE POSITION. HMA82GR7CJR8N-VK occurs exactly ONCE in the
//      machine. The firmware puts it at SK0 MC5; the SMBIOS table puts it at "DIMM 11". Under this
//      join those are the same connector, J11, and MCU5's odd member is J11. A wrong ordering
//      would have to place a one-of-sixteen part on the one position that agrees with the pair
//      table, which no shift or reversal of the sequence does.
//
//   2. THE RANK SEQUENCE REPRODUCES INDEPENDENTLY OF PART NUMBER. SMBIOS's own Rank field reads
//      1,2,1,1,2,2,1,2 across socket 0's populated slots and 1,1,2,1,1,1,1,2 across socket 1's;
//      the firmware's 1R/2R column reads the same two sequences in MCU order. That is a second
//      field, read by a different agent from a different table, agreeing position for position.
//
//   3. THE POPULATED SET IS EXACTLY THE ODD CONNECTORS, which is what the Getting Started Guide's
//      16-DIMM row independently specifies, and the firmware independently reports every module at
//      S0 — slot 0 of its channel — on all sixteen channels.
//
// WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED. This does not establish that the board silkscreen reads "J11" beside the
// eleventh socket-0 connector; no one has looked at the board. It establishes that the connector
// this repository calls J11, as Ampere's tables define it, is the one carrying the CJR8N module.
// A physical-label check is a photograph, not a software reading, and is out of scope here.
type MtCollins1ConnectorIdentificationBasis {
  smbios_to_connector_rule: NonEmptyStr
  discriminating_witness: NonEmptyStr
  corroborating_checks: NonEmptyStr
  not_established: NonEmptyStr
  upstream_pair_authority: DeclarationRef
}

data mtcollins1_connector_identification_basis: MtCollins1ConnectorIdentificationBasis = MtCollins1ConnectorIdentificationBasis {
  smbios_to_connector_rule: "SMBIOS Type 17 DeviceLocator \"DIMM n\" is connector Jn for socket 0 and J(16+n) for socket 1, in Type 17 structure order. SMBIOS prints no J-number; this is a join, not a reading.",
  discriminating_witness: "HMA82GR7CJR8N-VK occurs exactly once in the unit. Firmware places it at SK0 MC5; SMBIOS places it at \"DIMM 11\"; the Getting Started Guide's pair table places MCU5's 1DPC member at J11. A one-of-sixteen part agreeing with the pair table at the single position that carries it is not reproduced by any shift or reversal of the sequence.",
  corroborating_checks: "SMBIOS Rank reads 1,2,1,1,2,2,1,2 (socket 0) and 1,1,2,1,1,1,1,2 (socket 1) across populated slots in order, matching the firmware's 1R/2R column in MCU order field-for-field; and the populated set is exactly the odd connectors, which the guide's 16-DIMM row specifies and the firmware corroborates by reporting every module at slot S0 of its channel.",
  not_established: "That the physical board silkscreen reads J11 at that connector. No one has inspected the board; that is a photograph, not a software reading.",
  upstream_pair_authority: DeclarationRef {
    module_path: "extdeps.ampere.mt_collins_product_brief.memory_population",
    decl_name: "mt_collins_channel_connector_pairs",
    field: WholeDeclaration,
  },
}

// ── what the census does NOT answer, recorded so nobody re-runs the boot to look for it ────────

// THE CHANNEL MAP IS NOT OBTAINABLE FROM EDAC ON THIS PLATFORM and re-booting will not change
// that, because the cause is APEI firmware-first mode rather than a missing module or a boot
// option. Recording the negative WITH its mechanism is the difference between a fact and an
// unfinished errand: without it the next reader sees "no csrow tree" and schedules another boot.
// THE ROW THAT USED TO SIT HERE WAS A SIX-HUNDRED-CHARACTER PARAGRAPH IN A String, and review
// 64056 was right that it is the defect this module deletes three other rows for one screen
// below. Nothing folded it, and its real home was never here: the GHES/APEI mechanism is a fact
// about a Linux arrangement, not about this unit, and it lands as extdeps.linux.edac in
// gunbc#11071. What THIS module observed is kept and typed -- mtcollins1_edac_topology carries
// the driver, the firmware-first flag and the zero csrow count. The WHY is annotation until
// that carrier lands, and then it is a resolution against it rather than a paragraph here.

// ── the observation resolved against upstream authority ────────────────────────────────────────

// UNTIL TODAY THIS MODULE COULD ONLY NAME ITS GAPS. The part at J11 had no catalog row, and the
// same-channel mixing rule was in nobody's corpus, so the receipt recorded both as obligations and
// refused to author either from its own boot log. Both authorities landed -- gunbc#11066 from SK
// hynix Rev. 1.7, gunbc#11068 from the OCP Mt. Jade specification -- so the receipt now RESOLVES
// against them. That is the whole point of having kept the observation and the rule apart: the
// join becomes possible without anything being re-typed.
//
// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT BUILT HERE: a fill requirement or a purchase vector. This module
// resolves THIS UNIT's observations against cited rules. Turning sixteen per-connector
// requirements into an order is a decision over a population this module has not observed.
type MtCollins1PartnerRequirement {
  empty_connector: Nat
  peer_connector: Nat
  peer_part_number: NonEmptyStr
  rank_mixing_in_channel: MtJadeMixedDimmSupport
  width_mixing_in_channel: MtJadeMixedDimmSupport
  density_mixing_in_channel: MtJadeMixedDimmSupport
}

fn mtcollins1_mixing_verdict(placement: MtJadeDimmMixingPlacement, axis: MtJadeDimmMixingAxis) -> MtJadeMixedDimmSupport {
  match mt_jade_mixed_dimm_support(placement: placement, axis: axis) {
    MixedDimmSupportResolved { cell: c } => c.support
    MixedDimmSupportUnmodelled { query: _ } => MixingUndetermined
    MixedDimmSupportAmbiguous { query: _ } => MixingUndetermined
  }
}

// THE VERDICTS ARE THE SPECIFICATION'S OWN, CARRIED WHOLE. Same-channel rank, width and density
// each read MixingNotSupported, which is what makes each empty partner's required rank, width and
// density equal to its peer's -- a CONSEQUENCE of a cited rule rather than a preference read off
// the installed modules. The states are not collapsed to a Bool on the way through: a cell that
// said MixingNotPreferred or MixingUndetermined would arrive here saying so, which is the defect
// filed at gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.upstream_non_affirmation_consumed_as_permission.
fn mtcollins1_same_channel_verdict(axis: MtJadeDimmMixingAxis) -> MtJadeMixedDimmSupport {
  mtcollins1_mixing_verdict(placement: SameChannel, axis: axis)
}

fn mtcollins1_partner_requirement(b: MtCollins1FirmwareChannelBinding, peer_connector: Nat) -> MtCollins1PartnerRequirement {
  MtCollins1PartnerRequirement {
    empty_connector: peer_connector + 1,
    peer_connector: peer_connector,
    peer_part_number: b.part_number,
    rank_mixing_in_channel: mtcollins1_same_channel_verdict(axis: Mixing1rAnd2r),
    width_mixing_in_channel: mtcollins1_same_channel_verdict(axis: MixingX4AndX8),
    density_mixing_in_channel: mtcollins1_same_channel_verdict(axis: DifferentDensity),
  }
}

// THE CONFIGURATION THIS UNIT ALREADY RUNS IS NOT AFFIRMED BY ITS OWN PLATFORM SPECIFICATION, and
// that is recorded as a first-class reading rather than left for a reader to notice. Sixteen
// channels carry a mix of 1R and 2R modules ACROSS channels; the specification's different-channel
// row answers every other axis Supported and answers rank with MixingUndetermined -- its TBD.
//
// The machine boots and its EDAC counters are clean under load. That is evidence THIS SPECIMEN
// EXECUTES. It is not evidence that the authority affirms the arrangement, and substituting the
// first for the second is exactly the trap this row exists to keep open rather than close.

data mtcollins1_cross_channel_rank_mixing_standing: MtJadeMixedDimmSupport = mtcollins1_mixing_verdict(placement: DifferentChannel, axis: Mixing1rAnd2r)

// ── the population, in the corpus's own carrier ────────────────────────────────────────────────

// THIS IS WHAT THE CENSUS WAS FOR, AND IT COULD NOT BE WRITTEN UNTIL TODAY. HostMemoryPopulation is
// how gunbc.fleet.fleet_intent states what a machine carries, and mtcollins1 could not inhabit it
// while one of its three installed parts -- HMA82GR7CJR8N-VK at J11 -- had no catalog row to point
// at. gunbc#11066 landed that row from SK hynix Rev. 1.7, so the observation resolves into the
// same shape every other fleet host uses instead of staying a bespoke list of strings.
//
// THE STICK COUNTS ARE THE OBSERVED ONES, and they are joined to CATALOG IDENTITIES rather than to
// part-number spellings: ten AFR4N, five AFR8N, one CJR8N, sixteen in total across sixteen
// populated connectors. A future edit that renamed a part on both local readings would still have
// to survive resolution against the manufacturer rows these count.
data mtcollins1_host_memory_population: HostMemoryPopulation = HostMemoryPopulation {
  rows: [
    MemoryPopulationRow { module: hma82gr7afr4n_vk_catalog, stick_count: 10 },
    MemoryPopulationRow { module: hma82gr7afr8n_vk_catalog, stick_count: 5 },
    MemoryPopulationRow { module: hma82gr7cjr8n_vk_catalog, stick_count: 1 },
  ],
}

// ── what this census does NOT license anyone to conclude ───────────────────────────────────────

// AN EARLIER REVISION CARRIED THREE `data` ROWS HERE describing modelling work this census
// surfaced: a missing SK Hynix catalog row for HMA82GR7CJR8N-VK, and the missing OCP Mt. Jade
// same-channel rank rule. They were removed on an operator ruling, and the reason generalises.
// Nothing consumed them. A `data` row that no fold reads is a prose TODO wearing a type -- DESIGN
// section 3c's dangling declaration and section 4c's misplaced prose at once -- and giving a TODO
// a struct makes it look discharged while changing nothing. Work that needs doing is a lane, not a
// row in the receipt of an unrelated observation.
//
// WHAT REMAINS HERE IS THE PART THAT IS ACTUALLY ABOUT THIS UNIT: the readings above are a
// POPULATION, and the rules that turn a population into a REQUIREMENT are upstream facts this
// module resolves against rather than restates.
//
// THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO SAY THE OPPOSITE AND WAS LEFT STANDING TOO LONG. It asserted that the OCP
// same-channel rank rule was "carried by no authority in this corpus" and that "chip width is not
// closed at all" -- true when written, false the moment gunbc#11068 landed, and still sitting here
// while the section above derived same-channel rank, width and density from that very authority.
// A receipt that both consumes a rule and denies it exists is a meaning fork (DESIGN section 3),
// and the defect is instructive: the contradiction was introduced by fixing the model in layers
// and not re-reading the prose around the edit. Review 64092 found it.
//
// WHAT THE AUTHORITY NOW SETTLES, via mtcollins1_same_channel_verdict: same-channel rank, x4/x8
// width and density all read MixingNotSupported, so each empty partner's rank, width and density
// are FIXED by the module already in its channel. The width term the earlier probe closed by
// reading it off the installed modules turns out to be closed by the specification -- which makes
// that draft right by accident, and this module's later "unresolved" claim wrong by being cautious
// about a rule it had not yet been given.
//
// WHAT REMAINS GENUINELY OPEN is cross-channel RANK mixing, which the same table answers TBD and
// which this unit already runs; mtcollins1_cross_channel_rank_mixing_standing carries that
// non-affirmation rather than rounding it to permission.
//
// SO THIS MODULE AUTHORS NO REQUIREMENT AND NO FILL VECTOR. The derivation belongs in a lane that
// consumes cited authorities -- the Ampere connector/channel mapping, an SK Hynix catalog row for
// every installed part, and the OCP same-channel rule -- and emits one requirement per empty
// connector. A requirement computed that way cannot quietly acquire a term the way a hand-written
// one did; that is the whole argument for computing it rather than writing it down.


// ── the media status observation, WITHOUT the decoder ─────────────────────────────────────────
//
// AN EARLIER REVISION OF THIS MODULE CARRIED A DECODED STATE MACHINE for the controller's
// redirection_status, with a readiness rule and a refusal range. It has been REMOVED rather than
// refined, for two independent reasons.
//
// IT WAS A REUSABLE PROTOCOL DECODER LIVING IN A UNIT RECEIPT. A mapping from vendor status codes
// to meanings, plus a rule about which of them permits a reset, is neither an observation of this
// unit nor a fact this module may own: it is an extdeps.bmc.megarac catalog keyed to a firmware
// identity, and a caller policy on top of it. Leaving it here would have made this receipt a
// second authority for a question the migration is modelling properly.
//
// AND ITS PROVENANCE WAS PROSE. The mapping was read from source.min.js as served by this
// controller, and NO SUCH ARTIFACT IS COMMITTED. A sentence naming the file it came from is not a
// resolvable receipt; a reader cannot check it, which is the same defect as a hand-transcribed
// number. The observation therefore does not belong in an evidence PR without its bytes.
//
// WHAT SURVIVES HERE IS THE MEASUREMENT ALONE, because the transition itself was observed against
// the live controller and is evidence about this unit. It is carried as SAMPLES rather than a
// sentence: the reading is a timestamped sequence of two raw controller fields, and a prose
// rendering of it hides the named parts a consumer would fold (DESIGN section 2 -- a String leaf
// hiding named parts is anemic modeling; section 4c -- an event belongs in a typed carrier).
//
// THE STATUS AND INDEX ARE CARRIED RAW AND UNDECODED, DELIBERATELY. What any particular value
// MEANS is an extdeps.bmc.megarac catalog keyed to a firmware identity, and the policy over it is
// the boot-path migration's; this module draws no readiness conclusion. The decoder that used to
// live here was removed because its source (source.min.js as served by this controller) is not a
// committed artifact, and a sentence naming the file it came from is not a resolvable receipt.
type MtCollins1MediaRedirectionSample {
  offset_from_attach: Second
  redirection_status: Nat
  session_index: Nat
}

data mtcollins1_media_attach_observed_at: EpochMs = 1789079896528

data mtcollins1_media_redirection_samples: List<MtCollins1MediaRedirectionSample> = [
  MtCollins1MediaRedirectionSample { offset_from_attach: second(count: 0), redirection_status: 100, session_index: 255 },
  MtCollins1MediaRedirectionSample { offset_from_attach: second(count: 12), redirection_status: 100, session_index: 0 },
  MtCollins1MediaRedirectionSample { offset_from_attach: second(count: 15), redirection_status: 1, session_index: 0 },
  MtCollins1MediaRedirectionSample { offset_from_attach: second(count: 267), redirection_status: 1, session_index: 0 },
]

// The last sample is +4m27s. The status observed there held through the whole boot that followed;
// that is a statement about duration rather than a further sample, so it is annotation and not a
// Bool row -- an unconsumed flag restating what the final sample already carries would be one more
// dangling declaration, which is the defect this section exists to answer.

// ── why the second capture took five attempts, and the defect that caused it ───────────────────

// THE HOST WOULD NOT BOOT THE PRESENTED MEDIUM, AND THE REASON IS A FIELD THE BOOT-HANDOFF MODEL
// DOES NOT CARRY. IPMI boot parameter 5 encodes TWO decisions: the device selector, and the BIOS
// boot type (byte 1 bit 5 — 0 = PC-compatible/legacy, 1 = EFI). gunbc.machine_intake_oob_boot_handoff
// and gunbc.machine_intake_mtcollins1_actuate model the SELECTOR and have no concept of the boot
// type at all: `efiboot`, `boot_type` and `legacy` appear nowhere in that path. So every handoff
// this repository performs requests the device under the DEFAULT boot type, which reads back as
// "BIOS PC Compatible (legacy) boot".
//
// ON aarch64 THERE IS NO LEGACY BIOS BOOT PATH. A legacy-boot request for the virtual CD is
// therefore unsatisfiable, the firmware cannot honour it, and it falls through to its own boot
// order — PXE, which nothing on this segment answers, and then the UEFI shell. That is the
// observed behaviour on every failed attempt.
//
// A CONTROL VARYING THE BOOT-TYPE BIT ALONE THEN BOOTED. Device selector and persistence were held
// constant; only the boot type changed, and the next reset booted the census image and produced the
// capture this module carries. That is the scope of what was shown: the boot-type axis. It does not
// establish a persistence policy, and this module deliberately does NOT state which argv the intake
// should use — the boot-intent product, its one-shot projection and the admissibility of a
// boot-type/architecture pair are the migration's to model, not a unit receipt's to prescribe.
//
// THE MODEL SAID THE HANDOFF SUCCEEDED EVERY TIME, WHICH IS THE DEEPER DEFECT AND THE REASON THIS
// SURVIVED. oob_boot_handoff mints BootHandoffCompleted once the override reads back with the
// expected rendering and the power action is accepted. Both were true on every failed attempt: the
// selector really did say "Force Boot from CD/DVD", and the power cycle really was accepted. The
// arm confirms a PRECONDITION and reports it as an EFFECT, so a handoff that provably never booted
// anything is indistinguishable, in the model, from one that did. That is DESIGN section 5's
// specification-without-execution, and it is why a legacy/EFI mismatch could persist undetected
// across an entire intake lane.
//
// Recording the instance without the class would be the weaker half: whatever argv the migration
// settles on, a terminal minted from preconditions would report the next unsatisfiable request as
// completed too. Both are named here as observations; neither is fixed by this module.
// PRESCRIPTION HAS BEEN STRIPPED FROM THIS ROW. It previously carried measured_fix, which told a
// reader what argv to use, what the one-shot projection is, and what to do if the firmware demands
// persistence. All of that is reusable boot-intent policy and belongs to the migration; a unit
// receipt that also prescribes protocol becomes a second authority the moment the migration lands.
// What remains is what this unit was OBSERVED to do.
// THE AXES ARE TYPED; ONLY THE READBACK QUOTATION IS A String, and that distinction is the point
// of review 64056. The earlier shape carried four prose paragraphs -- including "what the control
// varied" and "what the model reported", which are CLAIMS and were unfoldable as sentences. They
// are now Bools a witness can join, so the two-field boot decision this lane uncovered is a fact
// the corpus can check rather than a story it repeats.
//
// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT TYPED HERE: the boot SELECTION itself. Device, firmware boot mode and
// persistence as a decision carrier land in gunbc#11001, and minting a second one beside it would
// be the fork DESIGN section 3 forbids -- this module would then answer the same question as the
// model that adjudicates boot requests. So the selector stays an exact QUOTATION of what ipmitool
// rendered, which is evidence rather than commentary, and the axes that DISCRIMINATE the failing
// from the succeeding attempt are carried as the Bools below.
type MtCollins1BootDeliveryObservation {
  boot_flag_valid_observed: Bool
  device_selector_readback: NonEmptyStr
  efi_boot_type_on_failing_attempts: Bool
  efi_boot_type_on_succeeding_attempt: Bool
  persistence_held_constant_across_probes: Bool
  model_reported_completed_on_every_attempt: Bool
}

data mtcollins1_boot_delivery_observation: MtCollins1BootDeliveryObservation = MtCollins1BootDeliveryObservation {
  boot_flag_valid_observed: true,
  device_selector_readback: "Boot Device Selector : Force Boot from CD/DVD",
  efi_boot_type_on_failing_attempts: false,
  efi_boot_type_on_succeeding_attempt: true,
  persistence_held_constant_across_probes: true,
  model_reported_completed_on_every_attempt: true,
}

// ── the unit binding, CLOSED by a later capture ────────────────────────────────────────────────

// THIS RECEIPT'S FIRST CAPTURE BOUND ONLY TO THE BOARD MODEL and said so. It carried the kernel's
// `DMI: FOXCONN Mt. Collins/Mt. Collins` line, which names a MODEL, and no SMBIOS Type 1/2/3, so
// the board serial appeared nowhere in its bytes; what tied it to unit 1 was the delivery path,
// which is provenance held by the author rather than a fact a reader can check inside the artifact.
//
// A SECOND CAPTURE CLOSES IT, and the closure is an EQUALITY BETWEEN TWO INDEPENDENT READS rather
// than a stronger assertion of the same one. The host, reading its own SMBIOS Type 2 under Linux,
// reports board serial 02030A800TEXFT02L. gunbc.machine_intake_mtcollins1_access_observation
// reports the same serial from the CONTROLLER's IPMI FRU, over a different transport, from a
// different agent, recorded days earlier. Host-side DMI and controller-side FRU agreeing on one
// serial is what makes this an observation OF A UNIT; either alone is an observation of a reading.
// THE ATTEMPT LABEL INSIDE THIS ARTIFACT IS NOT AN ATTEMPT IDENTITY, AND THAT IS A PRODUCER DEFECT
// rather than a transcription slip. The capture's own header reads "attempt=3" — but so does the
// FAILED run recorded at mtcollins1_attempt3_failure, whose artifact is committed as
// mtcollins1-attempt3-sol.log. Two materially different attempts, minutes apart, one with no host
// capture at all and one that produced these bytes, both answer to "attempt=3".
//
// THE CAUSE IS THAT THE PRODUCER CARRIED A LITERAL. The capture script hard-codes its own attempt
// number, so re-running it re-uses the identity instead of minting a new one; the artifact FILE was
// correctly never overwritten, but the identity inside it was. An attempt identity that a rerun can
// silently duplicate is not an identity, and joining receipts on it would merge two attempts with
// different outcomes.
//
// THE BYTES ARE NOT EDITED TO FIX THIS. They are what the machine produced, and rewriting a capture
// to correct its own label would destroy the only property that makes it evidence. So the label is
// recorded as unreliable, the model carries the distinguishing facts instead — different artifact,
// different digest, different wall-clock window, different outcome — and the producer's literal is
// named as the thing to remove.
// THE DEFECT USED TO BE RESTATED IN A String ROW BELOW THIS ANNOTATION, and its only reader
// asserted the sentence was non-empty -- DESIGN section 3c's own tell, a witness asserting a
// declaration exists rather than exercising it. The CHECKABLE content of that sentence is the
// distinguishing facts, and those are typed elsewhere and genuinely folded: the two captures
// carry different digests and different observed_at timestamps, and
// the_capture_windows_actually_distinguish_the_attempts asserts the ordering that makes the
// wall-clock window a real discriminator. The prose stays as rationale; the claim is executed.

// HOW THESE BYTES WERE ACQUIRED, STATED SO THE OBSERVATIONS CANNOT BE MISREAD AS A CAPABILITY.
// Every capture in this module was produced by hand: ad-hoc scripts staged on a fleet host, driving
// ipmitool and the controller's REST API directly. There is NO modelled producer that reproduces
// any of it, and one successful manual capture is not evidence that the intake system can take
// another. The observations below are exactly as strong as their artifacts; the ACQUISITION is
// exactly as weak as this row says.
//
// This matters for what may consume the module. The hw-first-host clause asks whether THIS UNIT
// passed named facts, and may read the observations. Nothing may read them as proof of a reusable
// capture capability, because that capability does not exist yet.
type MtCollins1AcquisitionStanding
  = HistoricalAdHocCapture { reusable_producer_absent: Bool }
  | ProducedByModelledOperation { producer: DeclarationRef }

data mtcollins1_acquisition_standing: MtCollins1AcquisitionStanding = HistoricalAdHocCapture {
  reusable_producer_absent: true,
}

// ── who consumes the rows this module declares, and by what route ──────────────────────────────

// DESIGN section 3c REQUIRES EVERY ADDED DECLARATION TO NAME ITS CONSUMER AND ITS EXECUTING ROUTE,
// or to be classified honestly as a declared frontier with its trigger stated beside it. Review
// 63839 found that this module fails that test for a set of rows, and it is right: the witness
// folds the slot and channel rows, the topology, the workload, the subject binding, the samples
// and the standing, and reads none of the digests or the narrative receipts.
//
// THE DIGESTS ARE NOT DANGLING BY OVERSIGHT; THEY ARE WAITING ON A CONSUMER THAT CANNOT EXIST YET.
// A digest's real consumer re-reads the artifact bytes and compares. That is an effect, the witness
// is pure, and the producer that would perform it is exactly what mtcollins1_acquisition_standing
// declares absent. So they are a frontier, and the frontier is declared rather than left implicit
// -- a pure witness asserting "this digest row exists" would be section 3c's own tell, a witness
// that asserts a declaration exists rather than exercising it.
//
// THE TRIGGER NAMES A CAPABILITY, NOT AN ARTIFACT, per section 4b(3): not "a verifier lands" but a
// capture producer that re-derives these artifacts and adjudicates observed-versus-declared digest
// as a typed outcome -- SUFFICIENT FOR every artifact this module declares, not merely the one it
// is first pointed at. When that producer exists, mtcollins1_acquisition_standing becomes
// ProducedByModelledOperation, its witness control flips red by design, and these rows acquire a
// real consumer in the same motion.
type MtCollins1ConsumptionFrontier {
  declaration: DeclarationRef
  awaited_consumer: NonEmptyStr
  trigger_capability: NonEmptyStr
}

fn census_frontier_row(decl_name: String, awaited: NonEmptyStr, trigger: NonEmptyStr) -> MtCollins1ConsumptionFrontier {
  MtCollins1ConsumptionFrontier {
    declaration: DeclarationRef {
      module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake_mtcollins1_memory_census_observation",
      decl_name: decl_name,
      field: WholeDeclaration,
    },
    awaited_consumer: awaited,
    trigger_capability: trigger,
  }
}

fn artifact_identity_frontier(decl_name: String) -> MtCollins1ConsumptionFrontier {
  census_frontier_row(
    decl_name: decl_name,
    awaited: "A modelled capture producer that re-derives the artifact and adjudicates observed against declared -- path, byte count and digest together, since a digest authenticates bytes and says nothing about which file or how many bytes were expected.",
    trigger: "The machine-intake capture producer capability: an operation whose stdout, stderr and exit status are redirected into attempt-owned immutable output, whose digest is computed by the same run that produced the bytes, and which is SUFFICIENT FOR every artifact this module declares -- not one verifier pointed at one file.",
  )
}

fn boot_delivery_frontier(decl_name: String) -> MtCollins1ConsumptionFrontier {
  census_frontier_row(
    decl_name: decl_name,
    awaited: "The boot-path migration's carriers: this is evidence ABOUT boot delivery -- the measured flags on failing and succeeding attempts, and what the model reported throughout -- and its consumer is the model that adjudicates a boot request, not this census.",
    trigger: "A boot-delivery model whose subject-level terminal takes a host-originating attestation as a constructor argument, SUFFICIENT FOR every machine-intake actuation that currently concludes from an accepted command. In flight as gunbc#11001; this row is consumed when that model reads these observations as its regression case rather than restating them.",
  )
}

data mtcollins1_consumption_frontier: List<MtCollins1ConsumptionFrontier> = [
  artifact_identity_frontier(decl_name: "mtcollins1_census_digest"),
  artifact_identity_frontier(decl_name: "mtcollins1_census_sol_digest"),
  artifact_identity_frontier(decl_name: "mtcollins1_census_evidence_manifest_digest"),
  artifact_identity_frontier(decl_name: "mtcollins1_host_capture_digest"),
  artifact_identity_frontier(decl_name: "mtcollins1_census_artifact_path"),
  artifact_identity_frontier(decl_name: "mtcollins1_census_sol_artifact_path"),
  artifact_identity_frontier(decl_name: "mtcollins1_host_capture_artifact_path"),
  artifact_identity_frontier(decl_name: "mtcollins1_census_byte_count"),
  artifact_identity_frontier(decl_name: "mtcollins1_census_sol_byte_count"),
  artifact_identity_frontier(decl_name: "mtcollins1_census_kernel"),
  boot_delivery_frontier(decl_name: "mtcollins1_attempt3_failure"),


]

data mtcollins1_host_capture_artifact_path: NonEmptyStr = "artifacts/bmc/mtcollins1-host-capture.txt"

data mtcollins1_host_capture_byte_count: ByteSize = byte_size(count: 270880)

data mtcollins1_host_capture_digest: ContentHash = Sha256Hash(Sha256Digest {
  hex: "a9b880c3ecd9ba611c1df9e7de1ab089839c5d8e92b743bf2e1bcd684100fd2a",
})

// CORRECTED. This previously read 1789428410000, which is 2026-09-14T23:26:50Z — nearly four days
// after the capture actually ran. The artifact's own first line says
// "GUNBC-HOST-CAPTURE mtcollins1 attempt=3 begin 2026-09-11T01:26:50Z", and this module's whole
// claim is that it states nothing the artifact does not contain, so a timestamp the artifact
// contradicts is the one kind of error it cannot be allowed to carry. 1789090010000 is that line.
data mtcollins1_host_capture_observed_at: EpochMs = 1789090010000

// EVERY SECTION OF THAT CAPTURE CARRIES ITS ARGV AND ITS EXIT STATUS, which is what makes a
// missing reading distinguishable from a zero one. All nineteen sections exited 0, so no field
// below is standing on a command that failed quietly.
type MtCollins1SubjectBinding {
  smbios_board_serial: NonEmptyStr
  smbios_system_serial: NonEmptyStr
  smbios_system_uuid: NonEmptyStr
  baseboard_product: NonEmptyStr
  corroborating_controller_read: NonEmptyStr
}

data mtcollins1_subject_binding: MtCollins1SubjectBinding = MtCollins1SubjectBinding {
  smbios_board_serial: "02030A800TEXFT02L",
  smbios_system_serial: "MXX2080619",
  smbios_system_uuid: "d5b80e46-903a-1000-01e6-4cd68a3cc128",
  baseboard_product: "FOXCONN Mt. Collins, board version 02030A800-600-G",
  corroborating_controller_read: "gunbc.machine_intake_mtcollins1_access_observation reads board serial 02030A800TEXFT02L from the controller's IPMI FRU. The host reads the same serial from its own SMBIOS Type 2 under Linux. Two transports, two agents, one serial.",
}

// THE FAILED ATTEMPT IS PRESERVED, WHICH IS THE POINT OF PRESERVING IT. Attempt 3 was built to
// close the binding gap above and to add a named memory workload with before/after EDAC counters.
// It never produced a host capture: the kernel loaded from the virtual CD and then panicked with
// "VFS: Unable to mount root fs on unknown-block(0,0)". THE ORIGINAL TEXT HERE CALLED THAT A
// MEDIA DROP AND IT WAS WRONG TWICE OVER, so it is corrected rather than quietly edited.
//
// It read the 100 -> 1 transition as the session dying. That reading was asserted, never evidenced:
// the transition is recorded raw at mtcollins1_media_redirection_samples, and what any particular
// status code MEANS is the status-code catalog's to say, not this receipt's.
//
// And the console refutes the drop directly. The captured sequence is GNU GRUB, then
// "EFI stub: Booting Linux Kernel", then "EFI stub: ERROR: Failed to load initrd:
// 0x8000000000000001", then the VFS panic. So the firmware DID select the medium, GRUB DID run
// off it, and the kernel DID start; what failed is the INITRD load, with EFI_LOAD_ERROR. A
// medium that had vanished could not have served GRUB and a kernel first.
//
// WHAT THIS ATTEMPT THEREFORE DOES AND DOES NOT ESTABLISH. It establishes an initrd-load failure
// on a boot that reached the CD. It does NOT establish why. The attempt reset the host while the
// observed redirection status was still 100, whereas the boots that produced a host capture had
// already reached status 1. That difference is recorded; whether it CAUSED the initrd failure is
// not established by these artifacts, and no mechanism is asserted for it here.
//
// ITS BYTES ARE COMMITTED ANYWAY. The recurring failure mode this whole census exists to answer is
// that a decisive observation gets produced by ad-hoc probing and then lost because the probe's
// output was never a receipt; a FAILED probe is exactly as easy to lose and is evidence about the
// delivery route's reliability, which is a fact the boot-delivery model needs. Attempt 3 also
// produced a srv2-side observation before the boot — an ARP reachability reading with a typed
// verdict against the expected MAC, and the boot artifact's digest — and that file stands on its
// own regardless of the host never booting.
//
// WHY THERE IS NO ATTEMPT 5. Re-establishing the media after attempt 3 degraded further: stop-media
// and start-media both answered HTTP 500 and the CONFIGURATIONS READBACK WAS UNCHANGED, so neither
// requested transition was established as applied — the HTTP status decided nothing. This controller is
// the ONLY out-of-band path to the M1 canary, and hammering a degraded remote-media subsystem to
// buy a subject binding and a workload is not a trade worth making against the risk of losing that
// path. The run was stopped, the boot override cleared to "No override", and the controller left
// verified healthy over IPMI (ARP REACHABLE at the expected MAC, mc info firmware 0.32).
type MtCollins1CaptureAttemptFailure {
  attempt: Nat
  intent: NonEmptyStr
  observed_failure: NonEmptyStr
  preserved_artifacts: NonEmptyStr
  why_not_retried: NonEmptyStr
}

// THIS ATTEMPT'S srv2-SIDE ARTIFACT RETAINS THE BMC CREDENTIAL IN argv, DELIBERATELY. Two
// ----ARGV lines in mtcollins1-attempt3-srv2-side.txt record
// `ipmitool -I lanplus -H 192.168.1.228 -U admin -P admin`. Review flagged that as a credential
// published to a public repository, and I redacted it. THE REDACTION WAS WRONG AND HAS BEEN
// REVERTED; the artifact is restored byte-for-byte to what the machine produced, digest
// e7673b74a9361453440364334d9223706284dc25d66c9f5bde1230c2ec5720ad.
//
// WHY IT IS NOT A DISCLOSURE. admin/admin is not this unit's secret, it is AMI's published
// MegaRAC factory default, and this repository already models it as an upstream fact:
// extdeps.bmc.megarac `megarac_factory_login` carries username "admin" and published_password
// "admin", and the field is NAMED published_password precisely because the value is public. The
// artifact therefore discloses nothing the corpus does not already state by design, and redacting
// it protected nothing while destroying the one property that makes a capture evidence. What IS a
// real defect is the producer passing -P on the command line where the modelled route uses -f with
// a password file; that belongs in the producer, not in a post-hoc edit of captured bytes.
data mtcollins1_attempt3_failure: MtCollins1CaptureAttemptFailure = MtCollins1CaptureAttemptFailure {
  attempt: 3,
  intent: "Close the subject-binding gap (dmidecode type 1/2/3), record per-section argv and exit status, and add a named memory workload with EDAC counters read before and after, so the health reading would be a load-based one rather than a snapshot.",
  observed_failure: "The host reached the CD's GRUB, started the EFI kernel, and then failed at the INITRD load: \"EFI stub: ERROR: Failed to load initrd: 0x8000000000000001\", followed by \"VFS: Unable to mount root fs on unknown-block(0,0)\". NOT a media drop — a medium that had vanished could not have served GRUB and a kernel first. The host was reset while the observed redirection status was still 100, whereas the boots that produced a host capture had already reached status 1; these artifacts do not establish whether that difference caused the initrd failure. No host capture was produced.",
  preserved_artifacts: "artifacts/bmc/mtcollins1-attempt3-sol.log (the console, carrying the panic) and artifacts/bmc/mtcollins1-attempt3-srv2-side.txt (the pre-boot ARP observation with a typed verdict against the expected MAC, plus the boot artifact digest). A failed attempt whose bytes are discarded reproduces the exact failure mode this census exists to end.",
  why_not_retried: "A further attempt refused before booting: stop-media and start-media each answered HTTP 500 and the subsequent configurations readback was UNCHANGED, so neither requested transition was established as applied; the HTTP status decided nothing either way. This controller is the only out-of-band path to the M1 canary, so driving a degraded remote-media subsystem harder was declined in favour of leaving the path intact. Boot override was cleared and the controller verified healthy over IPMI.",
}
